220
00:17:14,763 --> 00:17:17,708
in exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

221
00:17:19,548 --> 00:17:25,985
Q171: "HEAD, the index and the working
tree hold three different versions of one

222
00:17:26,025 --> 00:17:32,461
file. What does each hold after git reset
--soft HEAD~1, after --mixed and after

223
00:17:32,501 --> 00:17:35,943
--hard, and what does
git status -s print?"

224
00:17:38,283 --> 00:17:40,869
Answer out loud, and draw
the table as you speak.

225
00:17:45,709 --> 00:17:50,658
A strong answer derives each row from the
three steps of a reset, so that the table

226
00:17:50,698 --> 00:17:55,346
is the consequence of a rule and not a
list. It gets the status letters right,

227
00:17:55,386 --> 00:17:59,794
including the row where the letters
stay the same but mean something else.

228
00:17:59,834 --> 00:18:04,483
A follow-up will ask what became of the
versions that no place holds any more.

229
00:18:04,523 --> 00:18:07,849
Be ready to say, for each one,
whether an object exists.

230
00:18:09,689 --> 00:18:12,616
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

231
00:18:12,956 --> 00:18:16,842
A reset moves the current
branch and writes ORIG_HEAD.

232
00:18:16,882 --> 00:18:22,076
--mixed also rewrites the index, and
--hard also rewrites tracked files.

233
00:18:22,416 --> 00:18:28,284
Commits left behind are in the reflog, staged
content survives as unnamed blobs that

234
00:18:28,324 --> 00:18:34,193
git fsck --lost-found lists, and edits that
were never staged have no object and are

235
00:18:34,233 --> 00:18:34,545
gone.

236
00:18:34,885 --> 00:18:40,421
Before a hard reset I preview with git log,
git status -s and git diff HEAD, and I

237
00:18:40,461 --> 00:18:45,929
stash and branch if anything should survive.
To drop commits I type --keep, which

238
00:18:45,969 --> 00:18:51,574
carries my changes across or refuses. git
commit --amend is a soft reset plus a new

239
00:18:51,614 --> 00:18:54,226
commit, and it doesn't write ORIG_HEAD.

240
